From 80d751b16ccbc74b0fa317d9739a7d93c5d80140 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Viacheslav Bocharov <v@baodeep.com>
Date: Sun, 13 Sep 2026 15:14:05 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] fix(telegram): deliver inbound dice rolls to the agent

A dice message carries neither text nor a downloadable file, so it
normalized to an empty body and was dropped before reaching the agent.
Rolls made in a chat were invisible to it: no message, no note, no error.

Extract dice during inbound normalization the same way locations are
handled, and fold the result into the message body as a one-line marker.
Attribution stays out of the marker: the envelope already prefixes group
bodies with the sender label, and the inbound debounce buffer keys on
sender id, so a marker cannot surface under another user name.

The message cache and the reply-target projection derive their own bodies
from text, rich content, media, and location, so a reply to a roll reached
the agent without the roll. Move the shared shape into one producer:
resolveTelegramNonTextBody returns the body for messages carrying neither
text nor a downloadable file, and reports whether it is a location, which
counts as user text downstream and is forwarded structurally, or a dice
roll, which is neither. All three producers now use it.

hasUserText stays as it was. Its only consumer is the audio preflight, and
a Bot API message carries either dice or voice/audio, never both.

Upstream: https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/pull/137112
